out.push(h2('2.7  Review & Mediate: The reader who was copied in'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready – Unit 2 quiz'));
out.push(A('**1** _It is apparent that the second sample was taken in thirty-one, **which the table shows**._ _(the resumptive pronoun comes out)_ **2** _What the committee agreed was **that** the figures would be recounted._ **3** _We are not entirely confident, **which names nothing that is missing**._ **4** _The roller having been out, we cannot print **the order**._ _(the second reason comes off)_ **5** _Their disagreement about whether holding size explains it **is** not about the fall._ **6** _..., this makes the entry **harder** to obtain._'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**b** ⚠ **A copied-in reader is a reader who reads for what is missing, because it is the only reading available to somebody who was not written to.** **The one thing worth saying to the writer is a question: _what does each of these three readers do on Tuesday?_**'));
out.push(A('_(No Practise line in x.7. The Language box in a Review & Mediate cycle collects the three moves and carries no Practise item — the house pattern at every level of this series.)_'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the hardest mediation in the unit and the reason is that the writer is in the room.** **Mr Tayara wrote the letter and he put the deputy on the copy line out of decency, and the decency is what did the damage.** **A mediator who names that intention will be heard to be attacking it** — **so the Student\'s Book forbids it and the key says why: the fault is almost never where the writer\'s intention was.** ⚠ **The move that works is the question, and then the silence: _what does each of these three do on Tuesday?_** **Mr Tayara answers it himself — _two of them do nothing and one of them telephones me_ — and a finding about somebody\'s own letter only ever sticks when they have produced it.** **Count the silence: it runs eight or nine seconds and every instinct you have will be to fill it.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Pair A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**Move 1 — what is correct.** **It is a courteous letter, it is accurate, and it tells Mrs Haddad that somebody is dealing with it.**',
  '**Move 2 — the question.** **What does each of these three readers do on Tuesday?**',
  '**Move 3 — stop.** ⚠ _(count: in the recorded lesson it ran nine seconds)_',
  '**What the writer said at the end of it.** _"Two of them do nothing and one of them telephones me."_ ⚠ **And then, unprompted: _and the office has nothing to open a file with._**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('What each reader does on Tuesday, in both letters. ⚠ **Pair B is the one with the misplaced action**: _the batch should be re-tested_ **gives the work to a depot with no thermometer, and the passive is what hid it** — **nobody is named, so everybody assumes somebody else.** **Put the finding on the board: an action in the passive is an action nobody has been given.** **Then the harder half, which the room should supply: the writer of Pair B did not intend to give the depot anything at all.** **The sentence was a note to himself about what ought to happen**, and a note to oneself in the passive, in a letter with a copy line, becomes an instruction to whoever reads it first.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _**My copy line:** the regional pharmacist — note the eleven; the locum agency — **nothing**; the dispensary file — **nothing**._ ⚠ _**Decision: the agency comes off the line** (the locum made none of the eleven and the letter would tell him he was in the clear, which is 2.7 exactly). **The file stays and gets a verb: _to be held with the October sheets_.**_',
  '**b** _(doing the three moves on a partner\'s letter)_ _I asked the question and said nothing. **Seven seconds.** Then she said: "the second name has no action and I put him there so that he would not hear it from somebody else."_ ⚠ _**Which is the same reason Mr Tayara had, and she got there without my mentioning him.**_',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Two readings of one letter. Asked which reader is right, Mrs Rizk said both — and the second reading is the one that matters, because it is about a sentence that is not in the letter. Mr Shihabi asked her to say that again: an administrative oversight has been identified does not say who signed it, so the deputy read the letter and found out that he was safe. Nobody wrote that and he is not wrong to have read it. Mrs Oueidat said a copied-in reader reads for what is missing, always, because it is the only reading available to somebody who was not written to. Mr Tayara said he wrote that letter and had not thought about Mr Sabbagh reading it at all. Mrs Tarabishi pointed out that he is on the copy line, and he is on the copy line because he signed the form: Mr Tayara put him there to be fair to him, and the letter told him he was in the clear, which is the opposite of fair to Mrs Haddad. Dr Qabbani said and the third reader has nothing at all, and Mrs Khoury said the examinations office was copied in and given no date, no candidate number and no request — a copy line with nothing on it for the reader creates a file and no action, and in eight months nobody there did anything, which is exactly what the letter asked of them. Mrs Ajouz asked what you say to the writer, since he is in the room, and Mr Zahreddine said nothing about the oversight sentence, which is where everybody will want to start: ask him one question — what does each of the three people on this letter do on Tuesday? Mr Tayara answered it himself: two of them do nothing and one of them telephones me. Which is the finding, produced by the writer, and that is the only way it ever sticks. Mr Attar asked about the thing not to do, and Mrs Oueidat said do not tell him what the oversight sentence means to the deputy: he will hear it as an accusation about his own fairness, and it is not, because he put the deputy on the copy line out of decency and the decency is what did the damage. Mrs Khoury said that is the hard part of mediating a document — the fault is almost never where the writer\'s intention was, and his intention was to be fair to the deputy while the result was a letter that cleared the deputy and told a mother nothing. And Mrs Rizk said the three moves are the ones from Unit 1 with one word changed: say what is correct, ask what each reader does on Tuesday, and stop. The sentence that fixes the letter is the one he will write himself, in the silence.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the name on the student\'s own copy line with no action. ⚠ **Collect those names and ask, of each, why they were put there.** **Almost every answer is a version of _so that they would not hear it from somebody else_**, which is decency and is the exact mechanism of the fault. **Say it once, and then give the repair, which is one sentence long: a reader put on a copy line out of decency gets a verb — _should note that_ — or comes off the line and is telephoned.** **There is no third option**, and a class that leaves this cycle with that sentence has something to use on Monday morning.'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Explaining what _an oversight has been identified_ means to the deputy. → **✓** ask about Tuesday.   **✗** _You put him there to protect yourself._ → **✓** the intention is not the fault.   **✗** _The batch should be re-tested._ → **✓** by whom?   **✗** A copy line with three names and no verbs. → **✓** a verb each, or off the line.   **✗** Filling the silence after the question. → **✓** eight or nine seconds.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read – exam practice (Reading and Use of English Part 4)'));
out.push(A('**1** **What the committee did not agree about** _(6 words)_   **2** **have not been checked** _(4)_   **3** **has never been established** _(4)_   **4** **the roller having been out** _(5)_   **5** **a frame being certified** _(4)_   **6** **declined to leave** _(3)_'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Part 4 at C1 is two marks an item and the marks are divided**, so a half-right answer scores one. **Teach the three mechanical checks in this order.** **One: is the given word unchanged?** _DECLINED_ **may not become _declining_.** **Two: count the words, including the given one** — three to eight, and contractions count as two. **Three: read the whole second sentence aloud for sense**, because the commonest loss is a correct phrase that does not fit what follows it.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Two of these six items have a second accepted answer and the real examination would print both, so this key prints both and says so.** **Item 2:** _have not been checked_ **and** _had not been checked_ **are both accepted**, the original giving no time reference. **Item 6:** _declined to leave_ **and** _declined to take_ **are both accepted.** **Everything else on this page has one answer and the class should be told which is which**, because a candidate who believes every item has exactly one answer argues with the key and a candidate who believes every item has several stops checking. ⚠ **Item 4 is the one with genuinely one answer and it is the unit\'s own grammar: _the roller having been out_ is the absolute construction from Unit 1**, and nothing else fits five words after a comma.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
